            "original": "To avoid \"butchering\" your lines in a play, follow these tips:\n\n1. **Read your lines carefully**: Take time to read your lines multiple times, paying attention to the words, phrases, and sentence structure. Make sure you understand the context and meaning of the line.\n2. **Know your character**: Understand your character's personality, motivations, and emotions. This will help you interpret the line correctly and deliver it with confidence.\n3. **Get into character**: Connect with your character's experiences, feelings, and struggles. This will help you bring authenticity to the line and deliver it with conviction.\n4. **Use your own voice**: Don't try to imitate someone else's tone or inflection. Instead, use your own unique voice and expression to bring the line to life.\n5. **Pause for emphasis**: Pause briefly before delivering the line to allow for emphasis and to buy time for your fellow actors to react.\n6. **Listen to the director's notes**: Pay attention to any notes or feedback from the director, and incorporate them into your performance.\n7. **Stay focused**: Concentrate on delivering the line, rather than worrying about making mistakes. Remember, it's okay to make mistakes \u2013 it's all part of the learning process.\n8. **Practice, practice, practice**: Rehearse your lines in front of a mirror, record yourself, or practice with a friend or family member. The more you practice, the more comfortable you'll become with the lines.\n9. **Get feedback**: Ask your fellow actors or a director for feedback on your lines. Use their comments to identify areas for improvement and work on them.\n10. **Be patient**: Improvisation and acting take time and practice to develop. Don't be discouraged if you don't get it right immediately \u2013 keep working at it, and you'll see improvement over time.\n\nAdditionally, consider the following tips specific to reading lines:\n\n* **Pay attention to word choice**: Choose words that fit the cont